/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - An electronic control unit (ECU) is a small computer: a microcontroller with memory, input circuits for sensors, output drivers for actuators and a communication interface.
   - In the older point-to-point method, each signal has its own wire from the unit that measures it to every unit that uses it.
   - L is the number of links needed to join every one of n units directly to every other one.
   - Inside one ECU, chips talk over short board-level links, which some syllabi call communication protocols in embedded systems: UART (start and stop bits; also the basis of LIN), SPI (a fast clocked link to memory or driver chips) and I²C (tw
   - A cross-system function is a vehicle feature that no single ECU can perform alone.
   - Car makers group ECUs into domains by the kind of data they exchange: powertrain (engine, gearbox), chassis (ABS, ESP, steering, suspension), body and comfort (doors, seats, lamps, climate), infotainment (radio, navigation, phone, displays)
   - A vehicle bus is judged on a short list of requirements.
   - Because the bus is shared, its time is a limited resource.
   - the sum runs over every periodic message; bits per message is the frame length on the wire; the period is how often the message is sent (s); the bit rate is in bit/s.
*/
