/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Topology is the shape of the wiring between nodes.
   - A bus needs the least cable and is easy to extend; a star needs the most but isolates faults; a ring suits a continuous stream such as audio but needs every node working.
   - Network organization says who may start a transfer.
   - Addressing says how a receiver knows a message is for it.
   - The control mechanism, or bus access method, decides who may use the shared wire at a given moment:
   - The OSI (Open Systems Interconnection) reference model splits communication into seven layers.
   - CAN defines only layers 1 and 2; layers 3 to 6 are mostly empty, because control messages are short and stay inside the car.
   - SAE sorts vehicle networks into classes by data rate and the job they do.
   - Buses are also classed by application: diagnostic, safety (airbag sensors), multimedia and x-by-wire.
*/
